- **The docs' screenshots** (`docs/_site/src/assets/screenshots/`) are taken again from the one demo seed (`scripts/docs/screenshots.mjs`); none is taken from it yet. This goal.
- **Words to correct:** CONVENTIONS.md's "One name map" ("v0.1.1 keeps no old names"), the 2026-10-26 row of [decisions.md](../decisions.md) and the guide's Q7 and rule 3 predate the owner's decision of 2026-10-31 23:15, which keeps the in-place upgrade of a v0.1.0 control's data and the readers of v0.1.0 ledgers' older events. This goal.
- **Q17:** no goal or roadmap line holds the move to Node 24 in v0.1.2 yet.
- **The end's checks:** `npm run check`, the docs checks and the acceptance checks the changes need, with which ran (this goal's last open box, and goal 172's step 11).

## Decisions

- 2026-10-24, owner: the file-size rule goes in CONVENTIONS.md, and its large files need a better handle.
- 2026-10-24, owner: v0.1.1 is the code clean-up, to the engineering rules and the new conventions.
- 2026-10-24, owner: one goal for all of it, to run with `/goal` after v0.1.0 ships: file sizes, the code's structure, duplication and every other rule that CONVENTIONS.md checks. Goal [124](124-file-sizes.goal) is merged into it, and CONVENTIONS.md gets rules on duplication and dead code.
- 2026-10-24, owner: a read-only pre-audit, goal [127](_completed/127-clean-up-pre-audit.goal), comes first and writes the guide this goal works from: a double audit.
- 2026-10-24, owner: tests live in a `tests/` folder, not beside the code ("It makes reading code and finding actual code faster"); and folders and modules with proper imports, not files named `<name>-<part>.ts` side by side ("we just made tons of files putting hierarchy flat and hard to follow").

- 2026-10-26: the tests' move is done by goal [160](_completed/160-v010-audit.goal), so it leaves this goal's scope.
- 2026-10-26, owner: v0.1.1 breaks compatibility with v0.1.0 ([decisions](../decisions.md#releases-and-planning)): "its ok to say v0.1.0 things don't work in v0.1.1". The code for older data and older parts goes, and names change to the public ones everywhere, the stored and wire names included ("Do what is needed"). A ledger or database that v0.1.1 can't read is kept, never deleted, and the changelog says what an upgrade needs.
- 2026-10-26, owner: the guide's 26 questions are answered ([the guide, section 7](../code-clean-up-guide.md#7-the-owners-questions)). Every recommendation is accepted, except questions 7 and 11, answered as above. In short:
  1. The shared agent runtimes become a part of their own, `src/agents/`.
  2. The contract words (the kit schema, held-out words, goal values, research words and limits, model-route names, the rig settings schema) move to `core`.
  3. The import rule is a script in `npm run check` that fails, with today's exceptions listed by file.
  4. `core` does no I/O: the read of `package.json` moves to `common`.
  5. Event schemas are checked at compile time first, at control's intake later.
  6. The names from before v0.1.0 (`THERMITE_UNIT_ID`, `THERMITE_CONTROL_UNIT_TOKENS_FILE`, `--instance`, `--unit`, `--direction`, the reader for commands signed before 2026-10-15) go in v0.1.1.
  7. No compatibility with v0.1.0's data or parts (above).
  8. REST, MCP and the dashboard call domain modules directly; `ControlActions` goes.
  9. Control's store becomes sub-stores.
  10. The dashboard's browser scripts stay strings, with a lint, a parse test and constants injected with `JSON.stringify`.
  11. The public names everywhere in v0.1.1, stored and wire names included (above).
  12. Control shows the space each kind uses first, then prunes what it only copies or derives by settings that are off until set; the rigs' ledgers and the results repository are never pruned. The daily ledger copy stays daily, compressed, and pushed apart from the attempts. The judge may remove old builds, keeping the champions' and the newest few for each lane (a setting).
  13. A copy of the ledger and of `control.db` before every migration, by default, and `thermite backup` and `restore` for control's state, with a page.
  14. A remove is refused until the rig's results have left; a typed `discard <rig>` goes on, recorded in the audit log.
  15. Health routes check what each part needs, with a probe in every deployment; `/metrics` and Needs you are the alerts in v0.1.x.
  16. Logs at `info` by default; a rig's parts change level by a signed command, control by a setting; logs go to a table in `control.db` with FTS5 (goal 028).
  17. The images move to Node 24 in v0.1.2.
  18. Tests that wire several parts go to `tests/system/`; feature tests of control's pages to `tests/control/dashboard/`.
  19. The stand-ins move to `tests/stand-ins/<name>/`; the acceptance scripts get `// @ts-check`, and each an npm script `acceptance:<name>`.
  20. CI runs the judge's adversarial set and every acceptance check it can run on each version tag.
  21. One Containerfile, with a target for each image.
  22. Code comments give the reason in words, and a goal number only where the sentence needs it.
  23. The words of the guide's question 23 are taken, and the knowledge base's folders are collections; the paper, each answer card and the review panel's decisions become knowledge-base items.
  24. The research `search` tool and the contract's `search_policy` go; the advisors file's `kb` gives way to MCP servers that a kit names.
  25. `Ledger.invalidate()` is wired to an owner's command, in a goal of its own.
  26. Stops and resets reach the scheduler by a direct call too, and the events are still written.
- 2026-10-26, owner: the knowledge base's folders are called collections (the guide's question 23).
- 2026-10-26, owner: the guide's rules of shape are accepted: "these look mostly good". They go into CONVENTIONS.md first, in a commit of their own that the owner reviews, so every later step follows them.
- 2026-10-26, owner: each image upgrades the system packages of its base when it is built, so a rebuild takes the distribution's fixes. It goes in with v0.1.1's first code changes, beside goals 161 to 165, and the images are built again.
- 2026-10-28 00:08 UTC, owner, T7's three questions, from its design (one table of agent programs and one CLI trial loop): a Codex trial whose ChatGPT plan is full goes through the general provider-error path, as OpenCode on a ChatGPT route does, and Codex's own stop goes; Claude Code's limits note states its memory as measured in the real Claude Code acceptance, in place of OpenCode's "about 500 MiB"; an image without OpenCode's binary ends an OpenCode trial with a sentence that says so, as Claude Code's and Codex's do.
- 2026-10-31 23:15 UTC, owner, on T12 and T13 (each explained in plain words first): v0.1.1 keeps the in-place upgrade of a v0.1.0 control's data, so a control on v0.1.0 moves up keeping its data; only the readers and migrations for data from before v0.1.0 go, and the migration list is not squashed. What grows with history is kept by default, with settings to prune it that start off: control's copies of rigs' events and exports, the judge's build store and the advisors' records each get a setting, off until set, and a page shows the space each kind uses; only crash leftovers (temp folders) are swept, and a run's exports once pushed. The names from before v0.1.0 go now: `THERMITE_UNIT_ID`, the CLI flags `--instance`, `--unit` and `--direction`, and the reader for commands signed before 2026-10-15, with the changelog naming what replaced each. The rig image keeps a Containerfile of its own, built on a published harness image, rather than a target of the shared Containerfile.
- 2026-11-01 02:16 UTC, owner, on retention and exports (T12's step 7): users design their own retention policies, with full control over what each removes, where and when ("allow users to have full control and design over retention policies"), and by default everything is kept everywhere ("by default we keep everything everywhere"). This takes the place of one fixed setting per kind. Exports are made only when a run ends or when someone asks for one: the hourly exports and the daily ledger copy go ("No automatic exports should be triggered except for a job finish/completed"; "I hated that anyways and it serves no purpose for us"). An export is removed only by hand, from the Exports menu ("Users manually delete exports from the export menu"). A rig's outbox still empties once an export has left it, since control or the results repository then holds it; the exports that an owner's stop, terminate or full reset makes first stay, as part of that action, since they keep what it would lose.
- 2026-11-01 02:30 UTC, owner, on the retention mockup (a list of policies on Settings beside the Space table; each removes one kind, where the owner says, by a rule of age, of the newest few kept or of a size, by day, by week or only by hand; each starts off and shows what it would remove first; Run now asks first; what policies removed is listed and written to the audit log; Delete on the Exports page): build it as drawn. A policy may remove seven kinds: control's copy of the rigs' events, the judge's builds, the trials' session logs, the advisors' records, older versions of knowledge-base items, copies of control's store and the audit log; the first and the last carry a warning of what is lost. No policy removes exports, which go only by hand, nor the rigs' ledgers or the results repositories.
- 2026-11-01 04:50 UTC, owner, on the three kinds left after the first five were built: control's copy of the rigs' events becomes a kind a policy may remove, so control makes the bus's EVENTS stream again once, at the upgrade, without the setting that refused every delete and purge, and keeps for each rig a floor below which events were removed on purpose; rigs keep each trial's full session log after the trial ends, until a policy removes it; control keeps the two newest copies of its store by default, and a policy for that kind takes the place of that rule ("keep the two newest as default, policy can change it").
- 2026-11-01 06:44 UTC, owner, after a correction: v0.1.0 had no message bus, so no released control has an EVENTS stream that refuses purges, and a control that moves up from v0.1.0 makes the stream with the new settings. The one-time rebuild built for the answer above serves only a deployment that ran an unreleased build, which none did: it goes, with its part of the bus check. The events kind, its purge and the floors stay.
- 2026-11-01 18:19 UTC, owner, on the two names question 23 left open: the contract's `notes`, which the New campaign page calls "Additional information", becomes `additional_information`; the signed `direction` field of `assign_campaign`, the owner's own label for one run that no agent sees (`--note`, shown as "Note:" on the run's item), becomes `note`. Contracts and commands from before read under the new names, a contract keeping its keys as signed so its hash holds.
- 2026-11-01 18:33 UTC, owner, asked how to reach testing: finish the renames (question 11 and 23, and the folders and file names that go with them) in v0.1.1, then test; the sweeps that change no behaviour move to goal [178](178-clean-up-continued.goal) for v0.1.2 ("Finish the renames first"): goal numbers in comments, the unused exports, the dropped errors, the long files' cohesion, the 1,000,000-event measurement, the dashboard's log levels, the screenshots, Node.js 24 and pull mode's allow-list test.

## Open questions

- Whether the size check counts test files.
- Where a recorded reason lives: a comment at the top of the file, or a list beside the check's starting sizes.

